== 5. Where the ≈3 s of "server_processing" goes ==
The load probe polls job status every 500ms, and it drives a Chrome instance on the
same laptop while it does. Re-measured with a 25ms poll and no browser running:

$ node /tmp/where3s.mjs        # POST /api/jobs?slug=compress-pdf, then poll every 25ms
POST 202 at 15ms  (4888 bytes)      docs/qa/p1/multipage-fixture.pdf — the "ordinary" shape
     19ms  200 running
    512ms  200 completed

POST 202 at 52ms  (23325045 bytes) /tmp/perf-fixtures/large-fixture.pdf — 22778 KB, 340 pages
     56ms  200 running
   1010ms  200 completed

Ghostscript alone, the same two inputs, the exact argv from lib/server/toolProcessing.ts:
$ /usr/bin/time -p gs -dSAFER -sDEVICE=pdfwrite -dCompatibilityLevel=1.5 \
    -dPDFSETTINGS=/ebook -dNOPAUSE -dQUIET -dBATCH -sOutputFile=/tmp/gs-out.pdf <input>
  4888 bytes     real 0.09 / 0.07 / 0.07   (three runs)   ->    8218 bytes out
  23325045 bytes real 0.49                                ->  181469 bytes out

So: end to end 512ms and 1010ms; Ghostscript 80ms and 490ms of that. Both scale with
input size. The ≈3 s in section 1 is the probe's 500ms poll granularity plus
contention with the browser it drives — not a per-request server cost.

The path a real user takes does not poll at all: app/api/jobs/[id]/progress/route.ts
streams progress with a 400ms server-side poller.

$ command -v gs soffice qpdf pdftoppm pdfinfo tesseract ocrmypdf
gs /opt/homebrew/bin/gs · qpdf /opt/homebrew/bin/qpdf · pdftoppm ✓ · pdfinfo ✓
tesseract ✓ · ocrmypdf ✓ · soffice MISSING
   One binary of the seven is absent, and `/api/health/ready` ANDs all seven
   (app/api/health/ready/route.ts:62 `Object.values(deps).every(Boolean)`), which is
   the whole of this machine's 503. Compress ran on a real Ghostscript.

== 6. An unauthenticated GET of someone else's job ==
$ node /tmp/shape.mjs          # POST, then GET the job id with the session cookie DROPPED
POST 202 {"jobId":"cmtoqojgr000buoq9lu434wkr"}
GET  404 {"error":"Job not found."}
   Correct: jobs are owner-scoped and a stranger is told "not found" rather than
   "forbidden", so the endpoint does not confirm the id exists. Noted because the
   trace above needs a cookie jar to work, not as a finding — 11-legacy-job-ownership.log
   already covers the rule.
